(window.guideContent=window.guideContent||[]).push(["multiagent",{"c:when-multiple-agents-help":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A \u003cb>multi-agent system\u003c/b> runs several agents, each with its own loop, prompt, tools and context window, that cooperate on one goal. It wins when the work splits into \u003cb>independent parts that can run in parallel\u003c/b>, when the material is \u003cb>too big for one context window\u003c/b>, or when an \u003cb>independent judge\u003c/b> helps. It loses when the parts depend on each other's decisions. Either way it costs many times the tokens of a single agent, so the task must be worth it.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>What changes when you add agents\u003c/h4>\u003cp>In the Agents module, a single agent is a model calling tools in a loop with one growing context. A multi-agent system gives you four new levers:\u003c/p>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Parallelism.\u003c/b> Five subagents searching at once finish in roughly the time of one.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>More total context.\u003c/b> Each subagent has a fresh window. It can read 100,000 tokens of sources and hand back a 1,000-token summary, so the lead agent sees the distilled result of far more material than fits in one window.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Separation of concerns.\u003c/b> Each agent gets a focused prompt and only the tools it needs, which reduces wrong tool choices and lets each explore without being steered by the others' dead ends.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Independent judgement.\u003c/b> An evaluator agent that did not write the work grades it more honestly than the author grading itself.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>What the evidence says\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Anthropic's Research feature uses a lead agent that spawns parallel search subagents. On Anthropic's internal research eval, a system with a stronger lead model and cheaper subagent models scored \u003cb>90.2% higher\u003c/b> than the stronger model working alone. The example they give: finding the board members of every Information Technology company in the S&amp;P 500, which the multi-agent system split into parallel lookups while the single agent searched slowly in sequence and failed.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Their analysis of the BrowseComp browsing benchmark is the sobering part: three factors explained 95% of the performance variance, and \u003cb>token usage alone explained 80%\u003c/b>; the number of tool calls and the model choice explained the rest. In other words, multi-agent systems help largely because they let you spend more tokens usefully. The bill follows: in their data, agents used about \u003cb>4 times\u003c/b> the tokens of a chat interaction and multi-agent systems about \u003cb>15 times\u003c/b>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The case against\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Cognition, which builds the Devin coding agent, argued in 2025 that most multi-agent designs are fragile. Their example: split \"build a Flappy Bird clone\" into \"background with pipes\" and \"the bird\" for two subagents. One builds a Super Mario style background, the other a bird in a different visual style, and the final agent cannot reconcile them. Their two principles:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Share context\u003c/b>, including full traces of what other agents did, not just the messages sent to them.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Actions carry implicit decisions\u003c/b>, and parallel agents make conflicting decisions.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Anthropic reached a compatible conclusion: tasks where all agents need the same context, or with many dependencies between agents (most coding), are a poor fit today.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use which\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Favour multiple agents when\u003c/th>\u003cth>Favour one agent when\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>The task is breadth-first: many independent questions or sources\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Steps depend tightly on earlier decisions\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Material exceeds one context window and can be summarised in parts\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Everything must stay consistent (one code change, one document's style)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Different parts need different tools or permissions\u003c/td>\u003ctd>One tool set covers the job\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Independent review adds value (generator and evaluator)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The answer can be verified by a test or a checker\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Each task is valuable enough to pay several times the tokens\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Volume is high and margins are thin\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Task\u003c/th>\u003cth>Verdict\u003c/th>\u003cth>Why\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\"Compare the data-retention terms of 12 model providers\"\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Multi-agent\u003c/td>\u003ctd>12 independent lookups in parallel, each reading long legal pages, then one synthesis\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\"Fix the failing test in the payments service\"\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Single agent\u003c/td>\u003ctd>One coherent change across a few files; parallel agents would make conflicting edits\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\"Design a landing page and iterate until it is good\"\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Two roles\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A generator and a separate evaluator with a rubric; not parallel, but independent judgement helps\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Start with one agent\u003c/b>, measure, and add agents for a specific bottleneck (wall-clock time, context overflow, self-evaluation bias).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Compare at equal budgets.\u003c/b> Because token spend explains most of the variance, give the single-agent baseline the same token budget before you credit the architecture.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Most production \"multi-agent\" systems are modest: one orchestrator with read-only research subagents that answer bounded questions, while one agent does all the writing.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Name the conditions under which multi-agent beats single-agent.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>When the task is breadth-first and splits into independent subtasks that can run in parallel; when the information needed exceeds one context window and can be compressed by subagents; when subtasks need different tools, prompts or permissions; when an independent evaluator improves quality; and when each task is valuable enough to justify roughly 15 times the tokens of a chat (Anthropic's figure). It does not help when subtasks depend on each other's decisions or must share all context, which is why most coding tasks remain single-agent.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Anthropic found token usage explained 80% of performance variance on BrowseComp. What does that imply for evaluating a multi-agent design?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Much of a multi-agent system's advantage may come from simply spending more tokens. A fair comparison gives the single-agent baseline the same token budget; only the gain that remains is due to the architecture, and you must still decide whether the extra spend is worth it.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why did Cognition's Flappy Bird example fail?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The two subagents worked in parallel without seeing each other's work, so each made implicit decisions (visual style, game mechanics) that conflicted. The combining agent was left to merge incompatible parts. Shared context and a single decision-maker for coupled work would have avoided it.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:orchestrator-and-subagents":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>In the \u003cb>orchestrator-worker\u003c/b> pattern a lead agent plans the work, writes a brief for each subtask, runs \u003cb>subagents\u003c/b> in parallel, each in its own context, and combines their condensed results. The quality of the system depends mostly on the briefs: each needs an objective, an output format, guidance on tools and sources, clear boundaries and an effort budget.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The architecture\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>User question\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Lead agent\u003c/b>\u003csmall>plan, save plan to memory\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Subagents x N\u003c/b>\u003csmall>search in parallel\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Lead\u003c/b>\u003csmall>synthesise, find gaps\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>More research?\u003c/b>\u003csmall>loop or stop\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Citation pass\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Answer with sources\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Anthropic's Research system works like this:\u003c/p>\u003col>\u003cli>The \u003cb>lead agent\u003c/b> thinks through the question, decides how many subagents to use and saves its plan to external memory, because in their system a context beyond 200,000 tokens is truncated and the plan must survive.\u003c/li>\u003cli>It creates \u003cb>subagents\u003c/b> with specific research tasks. Each runs its own search loop, evaluates results, refines queries and returns findings.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The lead \u003cb>synthesises\u003c/b> the findings and decides whether more research is needed; if so it spawns more subagents or changes strategy.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A separate \u003cb>citation agent\u003c/b> matches each claim in the report to a location in the sources.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>Writing the brief\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Early versions let the lead write one-line tasks such as \"research the semiconductor shortage\". Subagents misread them or duplicated each other: one studied the 2021 automotive chip crisis while two others both investigated current supply chains. A good brief has five parts:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Part\u003c/th>\u003cth>Example\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Objective\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"Find the current data-retention period for API inputs at Provider X\"\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Output format\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"Return JSON: \u003ccode>{claim, quote, url, date}\u003c/code> per finding; at most 5 findings\"\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Tools and sources\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"Use web search; prefer the provider's own legal and docs pages over news\"\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Boundaries\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"Only API terms, not consumer apps; other agents cover Providers Y and Z\"\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Budget\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"At most 8 tool calls; stop early if you find an official statement\"\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Scaling effort\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Agents are bad at judging how much effort a question deserves; early versions spawned 50 subagents for simple queries. Anthropic embedded explicit rules in the lead's prompt:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Query type\u003c/th>\u003cth>Subagents\u003c/th>\u003cth>Tool calls\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Simple fact-finding\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1\u003c/td>\u003ctd>3 to 10\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Direct comparison\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2 to 4\u003c/td>\u003ctd>10 to 15 each\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Complex research\u003c/td>\u003ctd>More than 10, with clearly divided responsibilities\u003c/td>\u003ctd>As needed\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Two kinds of parallelism made the biggest speed difference: the lead starting 3 to 5 subagents at once instead of one after another, and each subagent issuing 3 or more tool calls in parallel. Together these cut research time by up to 90% on complex queries.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Spawning subagents as a tool\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The simplest implementation gives the lead a tool whose arguments are a list of briefs. Your harness runs one subagent per brief concurrently and returns their results as the tool result:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>SPAWN_TOOL = {\"type\": \"function\", \"function\": {\n    \"name\": \"run_subagents\",\n    \"description\": \"Run 1-5 research subagents in parallel. Each needs a complete, self-contained brief.\",\n    \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"required\": [\"briefs\"], \"properties\": {\"briefs\": {\n        \"type\": \"array\", \"maxItems\": 5, \"items\": {\"type\": \"object\",\n            \"required\": [\"objective\", \"output_format\", \"boundaries\", \"max_tool_calls\"],\n            \"properties\": {\"objective\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"output_format\": {\"type\": \"string\"},\n                           \"sources\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"boundaries\": {\"type\": \"string\"},\n                           \"max_tool_calls\": {\"type\": \"integer\", \"maximum\": 15}}}}}}}}\n\nasync def handle_run_subagents(briefs: list[dict], budget) -&gt; str:\n    results = await asyncio.gather(*(run_subagent(b, budget) for b in briefs),\n                                   return_exceptions=True)\n    return json.dumps([r if not isinstance(r, Exception) else {\"error\": str(r)} for r in results])\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>run_subagent\u003c/code> is an ordinary agent loop (as in the Agents module) with its own message list, a search tool and the brief as its first message. Note \u003ccode>return_exceptions=True\u003c/code>: one failing subagent must not crash the others. The lab at the end of this module builds the complete loop.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Choosing models\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A common and effective split is a \u003cb>strong lead\u003c/b> (planning and synthesis are the hard parts) and \u003cb>cheaper, faster subagents\u003c/b> (searching and summarising), which is exactly the configuration behind the 90.2% result. Subagents with simple jobs can often use a small model.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Synchronous versus asynchronous\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Anthropic's lead waits for each batch of subagents to finish before continuing. That is simple to build and reason about, but the lead cannot steer subagents mid-flight and the slowest subagent blocks everyone. Asynchronous designs, where subagents report as they finish and the lead spawns more on the fly, are faster but harder: result ordering, state consistency and error propagation all get trickier. Start synchronous.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Frameworks package this pattern: subagents in the Claude Agent SDK and LangChain's Deep Agents, agents-as-tools in the OpenAI Agents SDK, supervisor graphs in LangGraph (covered in the Graph engineering and Agent SDKs modules). The design questions are the same in all of them.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Make subagents \u003cb>read-only\u003c/b> where possible. Let one agent own every write.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Log every brief and every subagent result; most failures are visible in the briefs.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Design an orchestrator that spawns parallel searchers.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A lead agent receives the question, plans and saves the plan to external memory, decides from explicit scaling rules how many subagents to use (one for a simple fact, two to four for a comparison, more for broad research), and calls a \u003ccode>run_subagents\u003c/code> tool with one brief per subtask: objective, output format, preferred tools and sources, boundaries that separate it from the other subagents, and a tool-call budget. The harness runs the subagents concurrently, each with its own context and search tools, catching individual failures. Each returns condensed findings with sources. The lead synthesises, identifies gaps, optionally runs another round, then a citation pass attaches sources before answering. A global token budget and a maximum number of rounds bound the run.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why did vague briefs cause duplicated work?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Subagents cannot see each other. Without explicit boundaries, several of them interpret a broad task the same way and run the same searches, while other aspects get no coverage. Briefs must divide the work explicitly.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why use a stronger model for the lead and cheaper models for subagents?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The lead does the hardest reasoning (decomposition, judging gaps, synthesis) once per run, while subagents do many simpler search-and-summarise steps. Spending on the lead and saving on the high-volume subagents gives most of the quality at a much lower cost.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:handoffs-between-specialised-agents":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A \u003cb>handoff\u003c/b> transfers the conversation from one agent to another: the receiving specialist becomes the active agent, with its own instructions and tools, and answers the user directly. It is the right pattern when \u003ci>routing\u003c/i> is the job (triage, escalation). If the first agent should stay in charge and only borrow a specialist for a subtask, call the specialist \u003cb>as a tool\u003c/b> instead.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Handoffs versus agents-as-tools\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>\u003c/th>\u003cth>Handoff\u003c/th>\u003cth>Agent as tool\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Who talks to the user afterwards\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The specialist\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The original (manager) agent\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Control\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Moves to the specialist for the rest of the turn\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Stays with the manager\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Specialist sees\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The conversation so far (optionally filtered)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Only the input the manager passes\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Good for\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Triage, routing, escalation to a human or a higher-permission agent\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Bounded subtasks: translate, research, summarise, check\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Both can be combined: a triage agent hands off to billing, and the billing agent calls a \"policy lookup\" agent as a tool.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>A triage agent with the OpenAI Agents SDK\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from agents import Agent, Runner, function_tool\nfrom agents.extensions.handoff_prompt import RECOMMENDED_PROMPT_PREFIX\n\n@function_tool\ndef refund(order_id: str, amount: float) -&gt; str:\n    \"\"\"Issue a refund for an order. Amount in GBP.\"\"\"\n    return billing_api.refund(order_id, amount)          # your system\n\n@function_tool\ndef lookup_incident(service: str) -&gt; str:\n    \"\"\"Return open incidents for a service.\"\"\"\n    return status_api.incidents(service)                 # your system\n\nbilling = Agent(\n    name=\"Billing agent\",\n    handoff_description=\"Invoices, refunds, payment methods and charges\",\n    instructions=RECOMMENDED_PROMPT_PREFIX + \"\\nYou handle billing. Confirm the order ID before any refund.\",\n    tools=[refund],\n)\ntech = Agent(\n    name=\"Tech support agent\",\n    handoff_description=\"Login problems, error messages, outages and bugs\",\n    instructions=RECOMMENDED_PROMPT_PREFIX + \"\\nYou handle technical issues. Check incidents first.\",\n    tools=[lookup_incident],\n)\ntriage = Agent(\n    name=\"Triage agent\",\n    instructions=RECOMMENDED_PROMPT_PREFIX + \"\\nWork out what the customer needs and hand off. \"\n                 \"Do not answer billing or technical questions yourself.\",\n    handoffs=[billing, tech],\n)\n\nresult = Runner.run_sync(triage, \"I was charged twice for order 8812\")\nprint(result.last_agent.name, \"-&gt;\", result.final_output)   # Billing agent -&gt; ...\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>How it works underneath\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There is no magic: \u003cb>a handoff is a tool\u003c/b>. The SDK gives the triage model two extra tools, \u003ccode>transfer_to_billing_agent\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>transfer_to_tech_support_agent\u003c/code>, whose descriptions include each \u003ccode>handoff_description\u003c/code>. When the model calls one, the runner switches the active agent: the next model call uses the billing agent's instructions and tools, with the conversation history passed along. \u003ccode>handoff()\u003c/code> lets you customise this: an \u003ccode>input_filter\u003c/code> to trim what the specialist sees (for example, remove earlier tool calls), an \u003ccode>input_type\u003c/code> so the model must supply structured data such as an escalation reason, and an \u003ccode>on_handoff\u003c/code> callback to start fetching data early.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The same idea in plain code, if you do not use a framework:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>AGENTS = {\"triage\": TRIAGE_CFG, \"billing\": BILLING_CFG, \"tech\": TECH_CFG}   # prompt + tools each\nactive = \"triage\"\nwhile True:\n    cfg = AGENTS[active]\n    reply = llm(system=cfg.prompt, messages=history, tools=cfg.tools + cfg.transfer_tools)\n    if reply.tool_call and reply.tool_call.name.startswith(\"transfer_to_\"):\n        active = reply.tool_call.name.removeprefix(\"transfer_to_\")\n        history.append(note(f\"Handed off to {active}\"))\n        continue                                    # same history, new prompt and tools\n    ...                                             # normal tool execution or final answer\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Why specialise at all?\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Focused prompts.\u003c/b> Each agent's instructions cover one domain, so they stay short and precise.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Least privilege.\u003c/b> Only the billing agent has the \u003ccode>refund\u003c/code> tool. A prompt injection that reaches the triage agent cannot issue refunds.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Independent iteration.\u003c/b> You can evaluate and improve the tech agent without touching billing.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Ping-pong:\u003c/b> two agents keep handing the user back and forth. Limit handoffs per conversation and make the descriptions mutually exclusive.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Lost context:\u003c/b> an aggressive input filter drops the order number the user already gave. Filter tool noise, not facts.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Vague descriptions:\u003c/b> the model routes on \u003ccode>handoff_description\u003c/code>. \"Billing stuff\" routes worse than \"Invoices, refunds, payment methods and charges\".\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>No way out:\u003c/b> always include a handoff to a human (or a ticket) for requests no agent should handle.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Implement triage that hands off to billing or tech support: what are the parts?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Two specialist agents, each with a narrow \u003ccode>instructions\u003c/code> prompt, its own tools (refunds only for billing, incident lookup only for tech) and a precise \u003ccode>handoff_description\u003c/code>; and a triage agent whose instructions say to route rather than answer, with \u003ccode>handoffs=[billing, tech]\u003c/code>. Running the triage agent exposes \u003ccode>transfer_to_billing_agent\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>transfer_to_tech_support_agent\u003c/code> tools; when the model calls one, the runner makes that specialist the active agent with the conversation history, and \u003ccode>result.last_agent\u003c/code> shows who answered. Add a handoff limit, an input filter if needed and a human escalation path.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">When would you use an agent as a tool rather than a handoff?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>When the current agent should keep ownership of the conversation and the final answer, and only needs a specialist's output for a bounded subtask, such as a research summary, a translation or a policy check whose result it combines with other information.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does giving the refund tool only to the billing agent improve security?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It applies least privilege: the agents that read untrusted input first (triage) cannot perform the sensitive action, so a prompt injection or misrouting at triage cannot directly cause a refund. The refund requires reaching an agent whose instructions demand confirmation.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:context-isolation-and-shared-state":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Every agent has its own private context window. That \u003cb>isolation\u003c/b> is the point: a subagent can read 100,000 tokens of sources and return 1,000 tokens of distilled findings, so the orchestrator stays focused. The design work is deciding what goes \u003cb>down\u003c/b> (a self-contained brief plus the decisions it must respect), what comes \u003cb>up\u003c/b> (a small, structured result with sources, not a transcript) and where \u003cb>shared state\u003c/b> lives (files or a store that agents read and write by reference).\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Why isolation helps\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Compression.\u003c/b> Anthropic describes search as compression: each subagent explores, discards dead ends and condenses what matters. A subagent may use tens of thousands of tokens but typically returns a summary of 1,000 to 2,000.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Focus.\u003c/b> Long contexts degrade recall (context rot, covered in the Context engineering module). A subagent with a clean window does not carry the lead's 40 turns of history.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Parallelism.\u003c/b> Isolated contexts do not need locks; they run at the same time.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>A worked example: four subagents each read about 80,000 tokens of web pages and return 1,500-token summaries. The lead sees 6,000 tokens instead of 320,000, which would not even fit in many context windows, and it pays for those 320,000 tokens only once, as subagent input, instead of on every later lead turn.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The cost of isolation\u003c/h4>\u003cp>What a subagent does not see, it cannot respect. If the user said \"only EU providers\" in turn 3 and the brief omits it, the subagent will happily research US providers. Coupled subtasks also make conflicting choices, as in the Flappy Bird example from the first lesson. Hence the rule: \u003cb>put every constraint and decision a subagent needs into its brief\u003c/b>, and when subtasks are coupled, either pass the relevant decisions (or the full trace) or do not split them.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>What goes down: the brief\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>The objective, output format, boundaries and budget (previous lesson).\u003c/li>\u003cli>User constraints that apply (region, dates, audience, tone).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Decisions already made that the subagent must not revisit (\"we use PostgreSQL\", \"prices in GBP\").\u003c/li>\u003cli>Pointers to shared material by reference (\"the plan is in \u003ccode>run/plan.md\u003c/code>\"), not pasted in full.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>What comes up: the return contract\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Define a schema and a size cap for subagent results, and validate them like any tool output:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">json\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>{\n  \"objective\": \"Data-retention terms for Provider X API\",\n  \"status\": \"complete\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\"claim\": \"API inputs are retained for 30 days for abuse monitoring\",\n     \"quote\": \"We retain API inputs and outputs for up to 30 days...\",\n     \"source\": \"https://provider-x.example/legal/api-data\", \"confidence\": \"high\"}\n  ],\n  \"not_found\": [\"Whether zero-retention is available on the standard tier\"],\n  \"artifacts\": [\"store://run-42/provider-x-terms.md\"],\n  \"tool_calls_used\": 7\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Each field earns its place: \u003ccode>findings\u003c/code> with quotes and sources make the lead's synthesis and the final citations checkable; \u003ccode>not_found\u003c/code> tells the lead where gaps remain, so it can decide on another round instead of hallucinating; \u003ccode>status\u003c/code> separates \"done\" from \"ran out of budget\"; \u003ccode>artifacts\u003c/code> point to full outputs instead of copying them.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Artefacts by reference\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Long outputs such as code, full extracted documents, data tables and draft sections should not travel through the lead's context. Anthropic calls the alternative avoiding \"the game of telephone\": subagents write their work to an external store (files, object storage, a database) and return lightweight references. The lead, or a later writer agent, reads only what it needs. This keeps fidelity (no paraphrase of a paraphrase) and saves tokens.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Where shared state can live\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Option\u003c/th>\u003cth>How it works\u003c/th>\u003cth>Good for\u003c/th>\u003cth>Watch out for\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Messages through the orchestrator\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Subagents report to the lead, the lead passes on what others need\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Simple hub-and-spoke runs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The lead becomes a bottleneck and a paraphraser\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Shared files or artefact store\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Agents write outputs and read others' by path\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Research notes, code, reports\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Concurrent writes to the same file\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Blackboard\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A shared structured board (tasks, facts, open questions) all agents read and update\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Opportunistic, asynchronous collaboration\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Needs clear ownership and conflict rules\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Graph state with reducers\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Typed state object; each node returns updates merged by reducers\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Deterministic workflows (Graph engineering module)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>State schema design\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Long-term memory store\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Facts persisted across runs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Preferences and knowledge reused next time\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Stale or wrong memories (Memory module)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Concurrent writes\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Parallel agents that write to the same thing will eventually clobber each other. Common rules:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>One writer per resource.\u003c/b> Research subagents are read-only; one agent writes the report or the code.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Append-only logs\u003c/b> for shared notes, merged by the lead.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Optimistic locking\u003c/b>: read a version number, write only if it has not changed, otherwise re-read.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Separate workspaces\u003c/b> for parallel coding agents (git worktrees or branches), merged and tested afterwards.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Decide what subagents return to the orchestrator.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A small, structured result defined by a schema and capped in size: the objective it worked on, a status (complete, partial, budget exhausted), findings as claims with supporting quotes, sources and confidence, what it could not find, references to any large artefacts it saved to a shared store, and how much budget it used. Not raw transcripts or full documents. This lets the lead synthesise and cite accurately, see gaps and decide on further rounds, while keeping its own context small.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A subagent researched the wrong region even though the user had said \"EU only\" earlier. What went wrong and how do you fix it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The constraint lived only in the lead's conversation history, which the subagent cannot see; the brief omitted it. Briefs must be self-contained: include every user constraint and prior decision that applies, and consider validating briefs against a checklist before spawning.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why should a coding subagent write its patch to a file and return a reference instead of pasting the code into its reply?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Pasting routes the full code through the lead's context, costing tokens and inviting the lead to paraphrase or truncate it. A reference keeps the exact artefact intact in the store, and only the agent that applies or reviews the patch needs to read it.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:multiagent/communication-patterns-supervisor-hierarchical-s":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Multi-agent systems differ mainly in \u003cb>who decides what happens next\u003c/b> and \u003cb>how information flows\u003c/b>. A \u003cb>supervisor\u003c/b> routes work centrally; a \u003cb>hierarchy\u003c/b> stacks supervisors; a \u003cb>swarm\u003c/b> lets agents hand off to each other directly; a \u003cb>blackboard\u003c/b> coordinates through shared state; \u003cb>debate\u003c/b> and \u003cb>generator-evaluator\u003c/b> loops use agents to check each other. Pick the simplest topology that solves the problem.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The topologies\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Supervisor            Hierarchical                 Swarm (peer handoffs)\n    S                       S                        A &lt;----&gt; B\n  / | \\                   /   \\                       \\      /\n W  W  W                S1     S2                       \\  /\n                       / \\     / \\                        C\n                      W   W   W   W\n\nBlackboard                      Debate / generator-evaluator\n A    B    C                     A1 --proposal--&gt; A2\n  \\   |   /                      A1 &lt;--critique-- A2   (repeat N rounds,\n [shared board]                                         then judge or vote)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Pattern\u003c/th>\u003cth>Who decides next\u003c/th>\u003cth>Strengths\u003c/th>\u003cth>Weaknesses\u003c/th>\u003cth>Typical use\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Supervisor (orchestrator-worker)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A central agent\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Easy to reason about, budget and log centrally\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The supervisor is a bottleneck and paraphrases\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Research, multi-tool assistants\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Hierarchical\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Supervisors at each level\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Scales to many tools and domains\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Many hops, latency, cost, telephone effect\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Large enterprise assistants with many teams' tools\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Swarm\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The currently active agent, by handing off\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Specialist talks to the user directly; few hops\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Harder to bound; ping-pong risk\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Customer service routing, conversational flows\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Blackboard\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Agents pick work from shared state\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Asynchronous, flexible, agents can join any time\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Needs ownership and conflict rules\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Long investigations, incident response\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Debate\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A fixed protocol of rounds, then a judge or vote\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Catches reasoning errors and some hallucinations\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Cost grows with agents times rounds; can converge on shared mistakes\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Hard reasoning with checkable answers\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Generator-evaluator\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Evaluator's verdict loops back to the generator\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Independent, sceptical grading; steady improvement\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Each round is slow if the evaluator really tests\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Design, writing, app building\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Supervisor and hierarchy\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The supervisor is the orchestrator from earlier lessons. A \u003cb>hierarchy\u003c/b> groups workers into teams with their own supervisors (for example \"data team\" and \"communications team\") when one supervisor would face too many tools to choose well. Each extra level adds a model call per hop and another chance to lose detail, so add levels only when tool count or domain breadth forces you to.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Swarm\u003c/h4>\u003cp>In a swarm there is no permanent centre: each agent has handoff tools to the others, and whichever agent is active talks to the user. The system remembers the last active agent, so the next user message goes straight to it. It is the handoff lesson generalised. Bound it with a maximum number of handoffs per turn and clear, non-overlapping handoff descriptions.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Blackboard\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A classic AI architecture: a shared board holds the current problem state (hypotheses, facts, open tasks), and specialised agents watch it, contribute when they can and pick up open tasks. A controller (sometimes just a scheduler) decides which agent runs next. It suits long, exploratory work such as investigating an incident across logs, metrics and code, where you cannot plan the order of contributions up front.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Debate and generator-evaluator\u003c/h4>\u003cp>In \u003cb>multiagent debate\u003c/b> (Du et al., 2023), several model instances answer the same question, read each other's answers and reasoning, and revise over several rounds until they converge; the paper reports better mathematical and strategic reasoning and fewer factual errors than a single model. The cost is agents times rounds, and agents can still agree on the same wrong answer.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The \u003cb>generator-evaluator\u003c/b> loop is the more common production form. Anthropic's harness for long-running app building used three agents: a \u003cb>planner\u003c/b> that expands a short prompt into a product spec, a \u003cb>generator\u003c/b> that builds one feature per sprint, and an \u003cb>evaluator\u003c/b> that clicks through the running app with a browser tool and grades it against explicit criteria. The key finding: agents asked to judge their own work reliably praise it, and tuning a \u003ci>separate\u003c/i> evaluator to be sceptical is far easier than making a generator self-critical. In their front-end experiments each run took 5 to 15 generator-evaluator iterations and up to four hours.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Choosing\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Can a fixed workflow do it (Agents module)? Then no multi-agent system at all.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Parallel independent subtasks? Supervisor with subagents.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Routing a conversation to specialists? Handoffs (a small swarm).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Quality limited by self-assessment? Add an independent evaluator.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Hundreds of tools across domains? Consider a hierarchy, but first try tool search and skills (MCP module).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Open-ended, asynchronous investigation? Blackboard.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>Graph frameworks such as LangGraph let you build any of these as explicit graphs (Graph engineering module), and LangChain's documentation describes subagent, handoff, router and skills patterns with a table for choosing between them.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is the main difference between a supervisor and a swarm?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>In a supervisor design one central agent decides which worker runs next and gets every result back. In a swarm there is no central controller: the active agent hands off directly to a peer, which then talks to the user, and the system remembers which agent is active.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does a separate evaluator agent often improve quality more than asking the generator to review its own work?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Models grading their own output tend to be lenient and confidently praise it. A separate evaluator with explicit criteria (and ideally tools to really test the output) can be tuned to be sceptical, and its concrete critique gives the generator something specific to fix.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Name one risk of debate between several instances of the same model.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>They share the same training and biases, so they can converge confidently on the same wrong answer; and cost multiplies with the number of agents and rounds.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:progress-files-and-resumable-runs":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Long jobs outlive any single context window, process or deploy. Each new session starts with \u003cb>no memory\u003c/b>, like an engineer arriving for a new shift. The fix is to keep the state of the work \u003cb>outside the model\u003c/b>: a structured task or feature list, a short progress log, and git history, which every session reads at the start and updates at the end. Then any session can stop at any time and the next one picks up cleanly.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Why compaction is not enough\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Compaction summarises old turns so one session can continue. Anthropic found that even with compaction, a frontier model given \"build a clone of claude.ai\" failed in two ways: it tried to do everything at once and ran out of context mid-feature, leaving the next session to guess what had happened; and later sessions looked around, saw progress and \u003cb>declared the job done\u003c/b> too early. Some models also show \"context anxiety\", wrapping up as they approach what they believe is their limit. A \u003cb>context reset\u003c/b> (a fresh agent plus a structured handoff) avoids both, provided the handoff artefacts are good.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The two-part harness\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003cb>Initializer session\u003c/b> (runs once, with its own prompt):\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>Writes a \u003cb>feature list\u003c/b> from the user's request; in Anthropic's example, over 200 end-to-end features, all marked failing.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Writes \u003ccode>init.sh\u003c/code> to start the development server and run a basic end-to-end test.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Creates the progress file and makes the first git commit.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>\u003cb>Working sessions\u003c/b> (repeated):\u003c/p>\u003col>\u003cli>Run \u003ccode>pwd\u003c/code>; read the progress file and recent \u003ccode>git log\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Read the feature list and choose the highest-priority failing feature.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Run \u003ccode>init.sh\u003c/code> and a basic end-to-end check \u003ci>first\u003c/i>, so a broken state left by the previous session is fixed before new work starts.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Implement \u003cb>one\u003c/b> feature, test it end-to-end as a user would, mark it passing.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Commit with a descriptive message and append to the progress file.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>The files\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The feature list is JSON, and agents may change only the \u003ccode>passes\u003c/code> field. Anthropic found models less likely to rewrite or delete entries in JSON than in Markdown, and backed it with blunt instructions such as \"It is unacceptable to remove or edit tests\".\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">json\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>[\n  {\"id\": 17, \"category\": \"chat\",\n   \"description\": \"User opens a new chat, types a message, presses Enter and sees a streamed reply\",\n   \"steps\": [\"open /\", \"click New chat\", \"type 'hello'\", \"press Enter\", \"see reply\"],\n   \"passes\": false}\n]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The progress file is short, dated and factual:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>## Session 14 (2026-10-27)\n- Done: feature 17 (new chat + streamed reply). Verified in browser. Commit 9f3c2e1.\n- Broken: none. init.sh passes.\n- Next: feature 18 (rename chat). Note: rename API route exists but UI is missing.\n- Gotcha: dev server needs PORT=5173; tests flaky if run before DB seed.\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Wiring it into a harness\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A sketch; \u003ccode>run\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>ask_agent\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>progress_updated_since\u003c/code> are small helpers you would write around your agent SDK:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def start_session(repo) -&gt; str:\n    progress = (repo / \"progress.md\").read_text()[-4000:]            # most recent part\n    log = run([\"git\", \"-C\", str(repo), \"log\", \"--oneline\", \"-15\"])\n    todo = [f for f in json.loads((repo / \"features.json\").read_text()) if not f[\"passes\"]]\n    return (f\"{CODING_PROMPT}\\n\\n## Progress so far\\n{progress}\\n\\n## Recent commits\\n{log}\\n\\n\"\n            f\"## Next failing features\\n{json.dumps(todo[:3], indent=1)}\")\n\ndef end_session(repo, before_head: str) -&gt; None:\n    if run([\"git\", \"-C\", str(repo), \"rev-parse\", \"HEAD\"]) == before_head:\n        ask_agent(\"You made no commit. Commit working changes or revert them, then update progress.md.\")\n    if not progress_updated_since(repo, before_head):\n        ask_agent(\"Append this session's summary to progress.md: done, broken, next, gotchas.\")\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The harness, not the model, enforces the ritual: it injects the state at the start and refuses to end a session without a commit and a progress entry (in agent SDKs this is often a stop hook).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Beyond coding\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The same idea makes any long multi-agent run resumable. A research orchestrator keeps a \u003cb>run ledger\u003c/b>:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">json\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>{\"run_id\": \"run-42\", \"question\": \"...\", \"plan\": \"run/plan.md\",\n \"subtasks\": [{\"id\": \"s1\", \"brief\": \"...\", \"status\": \"done\", \"result\": \"store://run-42/s1.json\"},\n              {\"id\": \"s2\", \"brief\": \"...\", \"status\": \"running\", \"attempts\": 1}],\n \"tokens_used\": 183400, \"token_budget\": 400000}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>After a crash, the orchestrator reloads the ledger, keeps finished subtasks, re-runs the unfinished ones and continues. The next lesson covers making those re-runs safe.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Keep progress notes short and condense them periodically; a 50-page log is as unreadable to an agent as to a person.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Prefer machine-checkable status (tests, a \u003ccode>passes\u003c/code> field) over prose claims of completion.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Git commits are cheap checkpoints: an agent can revert a bad session with one command.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The Loop engineering module covers the companion technique of running an agent repeatedly in a loop against a plan file.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Make an agent write and read a progress log across sessions: how?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Create a progress file (and a structured task or feature list) in the workspace during an initial session. At the start of every session, the harness injects or instructs the agent to read the recent progress entries, \u003ccode>git log\u003c/code> and the next unfinished tasks, and to run a quick health check before new work. The agent works on one task, verifies it, commits, and appends a short dated entry (done, broken, next, gotchas). The harness enforces this by refusing to end a session without a commit and a progress entry, so the next session, with an empty context, can continue exactly where the last stopped.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why did Anthropic store the feature list as JSON with only a \u003ccode>passes\u003c/code> field editable?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>To stop agents declaring victory or quietly deleting hard requirements. Models were less likely to rewrite JSON than Markdown, and restricting edits to one boolean per feature (backed by strong instructions) keeps the definition of done fixed while progress is tracked.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is the difference between compaction and a context reset?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Compaction summarises older turns in place so the same session continues with a shorter history. A context reset ends the session and starts a fresh agent with an empty window, carrying state only through structured artefacts such as the progress file, feature list and git history. Resets give a clean slate (and avoid context anxiety) but depend on good handoff artefacts.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:durable-execution-and-checkpoints":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A long agent run will crash: a deploy, a rate limit, a provider outage, a timeout or an approval that takes two days. \u003cb>Checkpointing\u003c/b> saves the workflow's state after every step to durable storage, so a restart \u003cb>reloads the last checkpoint and runs only the unfinished steps\u003c/b>. Two things make that safe: side effects must be \u003cb>idempotent\u003c/b>, and non-deterministic results (model outputs) must be \u003cb>recorded\u003c/b>, not regenerated.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>What a checkpoint holds\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>The \u003cb>state\u003c/b>: plan, findings, messages, intermediate results.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Where you are\u003c/b>: which step or node runs next.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Pending writes\u003c/b>: outputs of steps that finished in a parallel batch where another step failed, so they need not run again.\u003c/li>\u003cli>An identifier for the run (a \u003cb>thread ID\u003c/b> or workflow ID) so you can find it again.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>The Progress files lesson did this by hand with files and git. Frameworks and durable execution engines do it automatically at every step boundary.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Resuming a crashed run with LangGraph\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A three-step research workflow whose search step fails once:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import sqlite3\nfrom typing import TypedDict\nfrom langgraph.graph import StateGraph, START, END\nfrom langgraph.checkpoint.sqlite import SqliteSaver      # pip install langgraph-checkpoint-sqlite\n\nclass S(TypedDict, total=False):\n    topic: str\n    plan: list[str]\n    findings: list[str]\n    report: str\n\ndef plan(s: S) -&gt; S:\n    return {\"plan\": [f\"{s['topic']} history\", f\"{s['topic']} today\"]}\n\ndef research(s: S) -&gt; S:\n    findings = [search_and_summarise(p) for p in s[\"plan\"]]   # may raise: timeout, 429, outage\n    return {\"findings\": findings}\n\ndef write(s: S) -&gt; S:\n    return {\"report\": \" | \".join(s[\"findings\"])}\n\ng = StateGraph(S)\ng.add_node(\"plan\", plan); g.add_node(\"research\", research); g.add_node(\"write\", write)\ng.add_edge(START, \"plan\"); g.add_edge(\"plan\", \"research\")\ng.add_edge(\"research\", \"write\"); g.add_edge(\"write\", END)\n\ngraph = g.compile(checkpointer=SqliteSaver(sqlite3.connect(\"runs.db\", check_same_thread=False)))\nconfig = {\"configurable\": {\"thread_id\": \"run-42\"}}\n\ntry:\n    graph.invoke({\"topic\": \"MCP\"}, config)          # crashes inside \"research\"\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(\"crashed:\", e)\n\nprint(graph.get_state(config).next)                 # ('research',): plan is already saved\nresult = graph.invoke(None, config)                 # None = resume from the last checkpoint\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Running this with a search function that fails on its first call prints \u003ccode>('research',)\u003c/code> after the crash, and on resume the \u003ccode>plan\u003c/code> node does \u003cb>not\u003c/b> run again: only \u003ccode>research\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>write\u003c/code> execute. In a real deployment the resume happens in a fresh process (after a restart, or from a worker that scans for unfinished threads), which works because the checkpoints are in SQLite or PostgreSQL, not in memory.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Side effects: the replay problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Resuming re-runs the step that failed, from its start. If that step had already sent an email or created a ticket before crashing, it will do so again. Make every side effect idempotent:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def create_ticket_once(run_id: str, step: str, payload: dict) -&gt; dict:\n    key = f\"{run_id}:{step}:{payload['title']}\"            # stable across retries\n    if (done := effects.get(key)) is not None:             # durable table of completed effects\n        return done\n    ticket = tickets_api.create(payload, idempotency_key=key)   # many APIs dedupe on this header\n    effects.put(key, ticket)\n    return ticket\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Also split steps so that each has at most one side effect, at its end. Then a crash before the effect re-runs harmlessly, and a crash after it is caught by the key.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Model calls are not deterministic\u003c/h4>\u003cp>If a crashed step is re-run, the model may now choose a different plan, so a resumed run can diverge from what the earlier steps assumed. Checkpointing each node's \u003ci>output\u003c/i> (as LangGraph does) means finished decisions are reused, not regenerated. Durable execution engines formalise this.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Durable execution engines\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Engines such as \u003cb>Temporal\u003c/b>, \u003cb>Restate\u003c/b>, \u003cb>DBOS\u003c/b> and \u003cb>Inngest\u003c/b> split your code into a deterministic \u003cb>workflow\u003c/b> (the control flow) and \u003cb>activities\u003c/b> (anything non-deterministic or with side effects: model calls, tool calls, API requests). Every activity's result is recorded in an event history. After a crash, the engine replays the workflow code, feeding it the recorded results instead of re-executing activities, until it reaches the point of failure, then carries on. Timers and waits (\"wait up to 3 days for approval\") survive restarts too. The Graph engineering module compares these engines; here the point is when you need one:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Option\u003c/th>\u003cth>Good for\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Hand-written ledger and idempotent steps\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Small harnesses, single process, few step types\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Graph framework checkpointer (LangGraph and similar)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Agent workflows with human approval, time travel and resumable threads\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Durable execution engine\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Business-critical, long-running processes with many side effects, retries and timers\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Managed agent platforms\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Teams that want a hosted harness to run and persist long agent sessions for them\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Multi-agent specifics\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Give every subagent its \u003cb>own thread or checkpoint namespace\u003c/b> (\u003ccode>run-42/s2\u003c/code>) and record its status in the orchestrator's ledger, so a crash re-runs only the unfinished subagents.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Make the orchestrator's \"spawn subagents\" step idempotent too: on resume it must not spawn a second copy of a subagent that is still running elsewhere.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Deploys while runs are in flight\u003c/b>: a new version of the prompts or tools can break a half-finished run. Anthropic uses \u003cb>rainbow deployments\u003c/b>, gradually shifting traffic to the new version while old runs finish on the old one. At minimum, store the code and prompt version in each checkpoint and resume with the same version.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Resume a crashed workflow from its last checkpoint: what do you need and what happens?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Compile or wrap the workflow with a persistent checkpointer (for example LangGraph with \u003ccode>SqliteSaver\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>PostgresSaver\u003c/code>) and run it under a stable thread ID. State is saved after every step. After a crash, inspect the saved state (\u003ccode>get_state(config).next\u003c/code> shows the step to run) and invoke the workflow again with no new input and the same thread ID (\u003ccode>graph.invoke(None, config)\u003c/code>); finished steps are not re-run and execution continues from the failed step. For safety, make that step's side effects idempotent with stable keys, and keep the code and prompt version the same as the original run.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why is an in-memory checkpointer not enough for fault tolerance?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It lives in the process's memory, so a crash or restart, which is exactly the failure you want to survive, erases the checkpoints. Use a database-backed checkpointer for anything that must resume after a process dies.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">In a durable execution engine, why must model calls be activities rather than workflow code?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Workflow code is replayed after a crash and must be deterministic. A model call returns different output each time, so it is run as an activity whose result is recorded; on replay the engine returns the recorded result instead of calling the model again, keeping the replay consistent.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:multiagent/cost-and-failure-modes-of-multi-agent-systems":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Multi-agent systems multiply tokens because every agent re-reads its own growing context on every turn, and they add failure modes that single agents do not have: misunderstood briefs, duplicated or conflicting work, agents ignoring each other, and nobody checking the result. Budget them explicitly (tokens, subagents, rounds, depth) and design against the known failure taxonomy.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Why tokens multiply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>An agent loop re-sends its context on every model call. If the context starts at C0 tokens and grows by d tokens per turn, T turns cost about:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>input tokens ~ T x C0 + d x T x (T - 1) / 2         (grows with the square of T)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>A worked comparison (illustrative numbers):\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Single agent, 10 turns, C0 = 2K, d = 3K:\n  input = 10 x 2K + 3K x 45 = 155K tokens, output ~ 6K\n\nOrchestrator + 5 subagents:\n  each subagent, 8 turns, C0 = 2K, d = 3K: 8 x 2K + 3K x 28 = 100K input, ~4K output\n  5 subagents: 500K input, 20K output\n  lead, 3 turns at 10K, 18K and 25K context: 53K input, 5K output\n  total ~ 553K input, 25K output: about 3.5x the single agent\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>That ratio matches Anthropic's observation (agents about 4x the tokens of chat, multi-agent systems about 15x). Now the money, with a strong model at $5 in and $25 out per million tokens and a cheap model at $1 and $5:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Single agent on the strong model: 155K x $5 + 6K x $25 = $0.78 + $0.15 = $0.93\nMulti-agent, strong lead, cheap subagents:\n  lead       53K x $5 + 5K x $25  = $0.27 + $0.13 = $0.40\n  subagents 500K x $1 + 20K x $5  = $0.50 + $0.10 = $0.60\n  total ~ $1.00, finished in roughly the time of the slowest subagent\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Three levers make the difference: \u003cb>cheaper models for subagents\u003c/b>, \u003cb>prompt caching\u003c/b> (each turn re-sends the same prefix, and cache reads are billed at roughly a tenth of the input price or less, so most of that quadratic input gets much cheaper; see the Billing module) and \u003cb>fewer turns\u003c/b> through good briefs and parallel tool calls.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Budgets that actually hold\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Budget\u003c/th>\u003cth>Example limit\u003c/th>\u003cth>Enforced where\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Total tokens per run\u003c/td>\u003ctd>400K, shared by all agents\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A shared counter checked before every model call\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Subagents per round and in total\u003c/td>\u003ctd>5 per round, 12 per run\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The spawn tool's schema and handler\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Rounds of research\u003c/td>\u003ctd>3\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The orchestrator loop\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Depth\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Subagents may not spawn subagents\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Do not give subagents the spawn tool\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Tool calls per subagent\u003c/td>\u003ctd>15\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The subagent loop\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Wall-clock time\u003c/td>\u003ctd>10 minutes\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A timeout around the whole run\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>A shared counter is a soft limit: subagents running in parallel can each make one more call after the budget is reached, so keep a reserve. When a budget runs out, return partial results with a clear \"budget exhausted\" status rather than failing silently.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How multi-agent systems fail\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Cemri and colleagues annotated more than 1,600 traces from seven popular multi-agent frameworks and built a taxonomy (MAST) of \u003cb>14 failure modes in three categories\u003c/b>:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Category\u003c/th>\u003cth>Failure modes\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>System design issues\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Disobeying the task specification; disobeying the role specification; step repetition; loss of conversation history; being unaware of termination conditions\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Inter-agent misalignment\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Conversation reset; failing to ask for clarification; task derailment; withholding information; ignoring another agent's input; mismatch between reasoning and action\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Task verification\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Premature termination; no or incomplete verification; incorrect verification\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Production experience adds a few named patterns:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Runaway spawning\u003c/b>: early versions of Anthropic's system spawned 50 subagents for simple questions and searched endlessly for sources that did not exist. Fix: explicit effort-scaling rules and hard limits.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Duplicated work\u003c/b>: vague briefs make subagents run the same searches. Fix: boundaries in every brief.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Conflicting edits\u003c/b>: parallel writers overwrite each other or make incompatible choices. Fix: one writer per resource.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Game of telephone\u003c/b>: results are paraphrased at every hop until details are lost. Fix: artefacts by reference, quotes with sources.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Ping-pong handoffs\u003c/b>: two agents keep passing the user back. Fix: handoff limits and exclusive descriptions.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Error cascades\u003c/b>: one failed tool sends an agent down a completely different path. Fix: tell agents when tools fail (they adapt surprisingly well) and checkpoint so you can retry from a known state.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Synchronous bottleneck\u003c/b>: the lead waits for the slowest subagent. Fix: per-subagent timeouts and accepting partial results.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Debugging needs traces\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Agents are non-deterministic, so \"it did not find the obvious answer\" can be a bad query, a bad source, a tool failure or a bad brief. Anthropic added full production tracing to see each agent's decisions, and monitored interaction patterns without reading user conversations. Record every brief, every model call with tokens, every tool call and every subagent result under one run ID (Observability module).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does an agent's token use grow faster than its number of turns?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Each model call re-sends the whole context so far, and the context grows every turn with new tool results and messages. Summed over T turns, input tokens grow roughly with T squared, which is why long loops and many parallel loops get expensive and why caching and compact tool results matter.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Give one failure mode from each MAST category with a fix.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>System design: step repetition (an agent redoes a search it already did); fix with a record of completed steps in its context or ledger. Inter-agent misalignment: withholding information (a subagent omits a constraint it found); fix with a return schema that requires all findings and caveats. Task verification: premature termination (the system stops before covering all parts of the question); fix with an explicit completion checklist checked by the lead or an evaluator agent.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your multi-agent research run must cost under $2. Which limits do you set?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A shared token budget (with a reserve for the final synthesis) computed from the model prices, a cap on subagents per round and per run, a maximum number of research rounds, no spawning by subagents, a tool-call cap per subagent, cheaper models for subagents, prompt caching for shared prefixes, and a rule to return partial results when the budget is exhausted.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:evaluating-multi-agent-systems":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Multi-agent systems take different valid paths to the same answer, so judge the \u003cb>outcome\u003c/b> (and the end state of anything they changed), not whether they followed a script. Start with about 20 realistic tasks, grade them with a rubric-based judge plus human review, run each several times, and compare architectures \u003cb>at matched token budgets\u003c/b> on success, cost and latency together.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Grade outcomes, not paths\u003c/h4>\u003cp>One agent might search three sources and another ten, and both can be right. Checking for a fixed sequence of steps would fail good runs. Anthropic's approach:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Outcome grading\u003c/b>: did the answer contain the right facts, with correct citations?\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>End-state grading\u003c/b> for agents that change things: is the database, repository or ticket queue in the correct final state? For long workflows, check a few discrete checkpoints where specific state changes should have happened rather than every intermediate step.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Process checks\u003c/b> as secondary signals: reasonable tool efficiency, no runaway spawning.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Start small, start now\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Early in development, changes have large effects; a prompt tweak can move success from 30% to 80%. Anthropic started with \u003cb>about 20 queries\u003c/b> representing real usage, which was enough to see such changes clearly. Do not wait for a 500-case dataset.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>A judge with a rubric\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Research answers are free text, so use an LLM judge (the Evals module covers judge design and calibration). Anthropic's rubric had five criteria:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Criterion\u003c/th>\u003cth>Question\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Factual accuracy\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Do the claims match the sources?\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Citation accuracy\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Do the cited sources actually support the claims?\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Completeness\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Are all requested aspects covered?\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Source quality\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Primary sources rather than low-quality secondary ones?\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Tool efficiency\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Were the right tools used a reasonable number of times?\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>They tried several judges for separate criteria and found a \u003cb>single judge call\u003c/b> producing 0.0 to 1.0 scores per criterion and an overall pass or fail was the most consistent and the best aligned with human judgement. When a test case has a clear answer (for example \"the three pharma companies with the largest R&amp;D budgets\"), the judge simply checks correctness.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Human review still matters.\u003c/b> Anthropic's testers noticed that early agents preferred SEO-optimised content farms over authoritative sources such as academic PDFs, something the automated eval had not caught; adding source-quality heuristics to the prompts fixed it.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>A fair comparison\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Same tasks\u003c/b>: 20 or more real questions, with reference answers or grading notes.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Several runs each\u003c/b>: agents are non-deterministic; run each task 3 times per configuration and report the mean and spread.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Matched budgets\u003c/b>: since token spend explains most of the performance variance, include a single agent given the \u003ci>same\u003c/i> token budget as the multi-agent system, not just the default single agent.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Measure everything per task\u003c/b>: pass rate, mean judge score, tokens, cost, wall-clock latency (p50 and p95), tool calls, subagents spawned.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Classify failures\u003c/b> using a taxonomy (the previous lesson) by reading traces of failed runs.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>Illustrative results for 20 research questions, 3 runs each:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Configuration\u003c/th>\u003cth>Pass rate\u003c/th>\u003cth>Mean cost per task\u003c/th>\u003cth>p50 latency\u003c/th>\u003cth>Tokens per task\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Single agent, 100K budget\u003c/td>\u003ctd>55%\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$0.30\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4 min\u003c/td>\u003ctd>95K\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Single agent, 400K budget\u003c/td>\u003ctd>63%\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$0.95\u003c/td>\u003ctd>11 min\u003c/td>\u003ctd>370K\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Lead + 4 subagents, 400K budget\u003c/td>\u003ctd>78%\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$1.05\u003c/td>\u003ctd>3 min\u003c/td>\u003ctd>390K\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>How to read it: part of the multi-agent gain (55% to 63%) comes from spending more tokens; the remaining gain (63% to 78%) and the much lower latency are due to the architecture. Whether that is worth $0.75 more per task than the cheap baseline is a product decision. With 20 tasks, a difference of one or two tasks is within noise; use more tasks or confidence intervals (Evals module) before you announce a winner.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Trace-level diagnostics\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Aggregate scores tell you \u003ci>whether\u003c/i>; traces tell you \u003ci>why\u003c/i>. For each failed run, check: did briefs carry all constraints, did subagents duplicate searches, were subagent results used in the final answer, did any agent stop early, did a tool fail silently? Count these over the dataset; the most common cause is your next fix.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Compare single- and multi-agent versions on success and cost: describe the experiment.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Take 20 or more realistic tasks with reference answers or grading notes. Run three configurations: the single agent at its normal budget, the single agent given the same token budget as the multi-agent system, and the multi-agent system, each task three times. Grade outcomes with a rubric-based LLM judge (factual accuracy, citation accuracy, completeness, source quality, efficiency) plus human spot checks, and record pass rate, cost, tokens, latency and tool calls per task. Report means with spread, attribute gains to budget versus architecture using the matched-budget baseline, read failed traces to classify errors, and decide whether the quality and latency gains justify the extra cost.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why grade outcomes rather than the sequence of steps?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Agents legitimately take different paths (different searches, tools or numbers of steps) to reach a correct result, so step-matching would penalise good runs. Outcome and end-state checks measure what matters, with process metrics such as tool efficiency as secondary signals.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your multi-agent system beats the default single agent by 15 points but uses 4 times the tokens. What else must you test before concluding the architecture is better?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Run the single agent with the same token budget. If it closes most of the gap, the gain came from spending more, not from the multi-agent design; only the remaining difference (and any latency benefit from parallelism) is attributable to the architecture.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","lab:multiagent/parallel-research-subagents-with-a-budget":"\u003cp>You will build the orchestrator-worker pattern in plain Python with no agent framework: a lead model plans subtasks, subagents search a local document collection in parallel with their own contexts, a shared budget stops the run from overspending, and the lead writes a cited answer. Then you compare it with a single agent.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Setup\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Python 3.11+, \u003ccode>pip install openai\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\u003cli>Any OpenAI-compatible endpoint (a provider, a gateway such as OpenRouter or LiteLLM, or a local Ollama server) and two models: a stronger one for the lead and a cheaper one for subagents. Both must support tool calling.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A corpus: a folder of 50 or more Markdown files you know well (your team's docs, or a public set such as a project's documentation). Put it in \u003ccode>./corpus\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">bash\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>export BASE_URL=https://openrouter.ai/api/v1  API_KEY=...  LEAD_MODEL=&lt;strong-model&gt;  SUB_MODEL=&lt;cheaper-model&gt;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Steps\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>1. Write the orchestrator\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Save this as \u003ccode>research.py\u003c/code>. Read it before running: the \u003ccode>Budget\u003c/code> is shared by every agent, each subagent has its own message list, and \u003ccode>asyncio.gather\u003c/code> runs them concurrently.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>\"\"\"Lead agent + parallel research subagents with a shared token budget.\"\"\"\nimport asyncio, json, os, pathlib, re\nfrom openai import AsyncOpenAI\n\nclient = AsyncOpenAI(base_url=os.environ.get(\"BASE_URL\"), api_key=os.environ[\"API_KEY\"])\nLEAD_MODEL, SUB_MODEL = os.environ[\"LEAD_MODEL\"], os.environ[\"SUB_MODEL\"]\nCORPUS = pathlib.Path(os.environ.get(\"CORPUS\", \"corpus\"))\n\n\nclass Budget:\n    \"\"\"One token pool shared by every agent in the run.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, max_tokens: int):\n        self.max_tokens, self.used, self.calls = max_tokens, 0, 0\n\n    def charge(self, usage) -&gt; None:\n        self.used += usage.prompt_tokens + usage.completion_tokens\n        self.calls += 1\n\n    def left(self) -&gt; int:\n        return self.max_tokens - self.used\n\n\ndef parse_json(text: str) -&gt; dict:\n    \"\"\"Models sometimes wrap JSON in code fences or prose; take the outermost {...}.\"\"\"\n    m = re.search(r\"\\{.*\\}\", text or \"\", re.S)\n    return json.loads(m.group(0)) if m else {}\n\n\ndef search(query: str) -&gt; str:\n    words = [w for w in re.findall(r\"\\w+\", query.lower()) if len(w) &gt; 2]\n    scored = []\n    for f in CORPUS.rglob(\"*.md\"):\n        text = f.read_text(errors=\"ignore\")\n        score = sum(text.lower().count(w) for w in words)\n        if score:\n            scored.append((score, str(f), text[:200].replace(\"\\n\", \" \")))\n    return json.dumps([{\"path\": p, \"snippet\": s} for _, p, s in sorted(scored, reverse=True)[:5]])\n\n\ndef read(path: str) -&gt; str:\n    f = pathlib.Path(path).resolve()\n    if CORPUS.resolve() not in f.parents:\n        return \"error: path outside corpus\"\n    return f.read_text(errors=\"ignore\")[:3000]\n\n\nTOOLS = [\n    {\"type\": \"function\", \"function\": {\"name\": \"search\", \"description\": \"Keyword search over the corpus; returns paths and snippets.\",\n     \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"query\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"query\"]}}},\n    {\"type\": \"function\", \"function\": {\"name\": \"read\", \"description\": \"Read the first 3,000 characters of a file returned by search.\",\n     \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"path\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"path\"]}}},\n]\nIMPL = {\"search\": search, \"read\": read}\n\nSUB_PROMPT = \"\"\"You are a research subagent. Objective: {objective}\nScope: {scope}. Use at most {max_calls} tool calls, then stop.\nReturn ONLY JSON: {{\"findings\": [{{\"claim\": \"...\", \"source\": \"&lt;path&gt;\"}}], \"open_questions\": [\"...\"]}}\"\"\"\n\n\nasync def subagent(brief: dict, budget: Budget, reserve: int = 4000) -&gt; dict:\n    messages = [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": SUB_PROMPT.format(**brief)}]\n    for _ in range(int(brief[\"max_calls\"]) + 1):\n        if budget.left() &lt; reserve:\n            return {\"findings\": [], \"open_questions\": [f\"budget exhausted: {brief['objective']}\"]}\n        r = await client.chat.completions.create(model=SUB_MODEL, messages=messages, tools=TOOLS)\n        budget.charge(r.usage)\n        msg = r.choices[0].message\n        if not msg.tool_calls:\n            try:\n                return parse_json(msg.content) or {\"findings\": [], \"open_questions\": [\"empty output\"]}\n            except json.JSONDecodeError:\n                return {\"findings\": [], \"open_questions\": [\"unparseable subagent output\"]}\n        messages.append(msg)\n        for tc in msg.tool_calls:\n            fn = IMPL.get(tc.function.name)\n            result = fn(**json.loads(tc.function.arguments)) if fn else \"error: unknown tool\"\n            messages.append({\"role\": \"tool\", \"tool_call_id\": tc.id, \"content\": result})\n    return {\"findings\": [], \"open_questions\": [\"step limit reached\"]}\n\n\nasync def synthesise(question: str, notes: str, budget: Budget) -&gt; str:\n    final = await client.chat.completions.create(model=LEAD_MODEL, messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\":\n        f\"Question: {question}\\n\\nResearch findings (JSON):\\n{notes}\\n\\n\"\n        \"Write a concise answer. Cite sources as [path] after each claim. \"\n        \"Use only the findings; list unanswered open questions at the end.\"}])\n    budget.charge(final.usage)\n    return final.choices[0].message.content\n\n\nasync def research(question: str, max_tokens: int = 60_000) -&gt; tuple[str, Budget]:\n    budget = Budget(max_tokens)\n    plan = await client.chat.completions.create(model=LEAD_MODEL, messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\":\n        \"Split this question into 2-4 independent research subtasks with non-overlapping scopes. \"\n        'Return ONLY JSON: {\"subtasks\": [{\"objective\": \"...\", \"scope\": \"what to cover and what to skip\", '\n        f'\"max_calls\": 4}}]}}\\n\\nQuestion: {question}'}])\n    budget.charge(plan.usage)\n    briefs = parse_json(plan.choices[0].message.content)[\"subtasks\"][:4]\n    results = await asyncio.gather(*(subagent(b, budget) for b in briefs))\n    notes = json.dumps([{\"objective\": b[\"objective\"], **r} for b, r in zip(briefs, results)], indent=1)\n    return await synthesise(question, notes, budget), budget\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    import sys, time\n    t = time.perf_counter()\n    answer, budget = asyncio.run(research(\" \".join(sys.argv[1:])))\n    print(answer)\n    print(f\"\\n{budget.used:,} of {budget.max_tokens:,} tokens, {budget.calls} model calls, \"\n          f\"{time.perf_counter() - t:.1f} s\")\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch5>2. Run it\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Pick a question that genuinely has several independent parts in your corpus, for example \"How do authentication, rate limits and pagination work in this API?\".\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">bash\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>python research.py \"How do authentication, rate limits and pagination work in this API?\"\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Print the briefs too (add \u003ccode>print(json.dumps(briefs, indent=1))\u003c/code> after planning) and check whether their scopes overlap.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>3. Squeeze the budget\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Run the same question with \u003ccode>max_tokens=8_000\u003c/code> (change the default or add a command-line flag). Watch subagents return \u003ccode>budget exhausted\u003c/code> and see how the final answer reports what it could not cover, instead of crashing or silently guessing.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>4. Add a single-agent baseline\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Add a function that gives one agent the whole question, with a larger tool-call allowance and the same token budget, then uses the same synthesis step:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>async def single(question: str, max_tokens: int = 60_000) -&gt; tuple[str, Budget]:\n    budget = Budget(max_tokens)\n    r = await subagent({\"objective\": question, \"scope\": \"everything relevant\", \"max_calls\": 12},\n                       budget, reserve=2000)\n    return await synthesise(question, json.dumps([r], indent=1), budget), budget\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch5>5. Compare\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Run both on three or four multi-part questions. For each run, record: number of distinct sources cited, whether every part of the question was answered, tokens used, model calls and wall-clock seconds. Put the results in a small table.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>What you should see\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>The multi-agent run is usually faster in wall-clock time because subagents search in parallel, and it often cites more distinct sources.\u003c/li>\u003cli>It uses more tokens than the single agent for the same question unless the budget binds.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Overlapping briefs show up as duplicate searches and duplicate citations; tightening the planning prompt (\"non-overlapping scopes\") reduces them.\u003c/li>\u003cli>With a tight budget, a well-built system degrades gracefully: partial answers with explicit open questions.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why can the run use slightly more than \u003ccode>max_tokens\u003c/code>?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The budget is checked before each call, but several subagents run concurrently; each can pass the check and make one more call before the counter updates. The \u003ccode>reserve\u003c/code> absorbs this overshoot, and a hard limit would need to reserve tokens before each call rather than charge them afterwards.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What change would most likely make subagents duplicate less work?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Better briefs from the lead: explicit, non-overlapping scopes that say what each subagent covers and what it should skip (which other subagents are handling), as in Anthropic's \"teach the orchestrator how to delegate\" principle.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","b:deep-research-agent":"\u003ch4>The brief\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Build a deep research agent: given a question, it plans, runs parallel search subagents, synthesises a report in which every claim has a citation backed by a quoted source, survives being killed halfway through, stays within a budget, and is evaluated honestly against a single-agent baseline. This is a standalone build that grows the lab into a real system. If you have Atlas, give the subagents Atlas's MCP \u003ccode>search_docs\u003c/code> tool as one of their sources alongside web search.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Requirements\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Sources\u003c/b>: a web search tool (a search API or your provider's built-in web search) and a page fetcher; optionally your own corpus over MCP.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Lead agent\u003c/b> on a strong model; \u003cb>subagents\u003c/b> on a cheaper model; subagents cannot spawn subagents.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Briefs\u003c/b> validated against a schema (objective, output format, sources, boundaries, budget) and \u003cb>effort-scaling rules\u003c/b> in the lead's prompt (simple, comparison, broad).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Subagent results\u003c/b> follow a return schema: findings with claim, exact quote, URL and confidence, plus what was not found.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Citations\u003c/b>: a final citation pass that checks each cited quote actually appears in the fetched source text, and removes or flags claims that fail.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Budgets\u003c/b>: shared token budget, caps on subagents per round and per run, maximum research rounds, per-subagent tool-call caps, and a wall-clock timeout; partial results reported when a budget runs out.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Resumability\u003c/b>: a run ledger (or a graph checkpointer) persisted to disk; \u003ccode>python deep.py --resume &lt;run_id&gt;\u003c/code> continues a killed run without re-running finished subagents.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Observability\u003c/b>: one log line or trace span per model call and tool call with run ID, agent, tokens, latency and cost.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Evaluation\u003c/b>: 20 questions with reference notes; single agent at default budget, single agent at matched budget and multi-agent system, 3 runs each; a rubric judge plus your own spot checks.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Milestones\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Port the lab code to real web search and page fetching; add the return schema and validate subagent outputs.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Add effort-scaling rules and up to three research rounds, with the lead deciding whether gaps remain.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Add the citation-verification pass and make the final report list sources with the quotes that support each claim.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Add budgets and the run ledger; test resumption by killing the process during a round (\u003ccode>kill -9\u003c/code>) and resuming.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Add structured logging or tracing and a per-run cost summary.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Write the 20-question eval set and the judge (factual accuracy, citation accuracy, completeness, source quality, efficiency; scores from 0 to 1 plus pass or fail).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Run the three configurations, read the traces of failures, classify them, make one targeted fix and rerun.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Write the report: results table, attribution of gains to budget versus architecture, failure analysis, cost per question, and when you would or would not use this design.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>Done when\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>A killed run resumes from its ledger without repeating completed subagents.\u003c/li>\u003cli>No final report contains a citation whose quote is missing from its source.\u003c/li>\u003cli>No run exceeds its token budget by more than the reserve.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The report compares all three configurations on pass rate, cost, tokens and latency with spread across runs, and states clearly what part of any gain is due to the architecture.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Stretch goals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Asynchronous subagents: the lead reads results as they arrive and spawns follow-ups without waiting for the whole batch.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Prompt caching for the shared subagent instructions, with the saving measured.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A separate evaluator agent that reviews the draft report against the question before it is returned.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Expose the whole agent as an MCP tool (with the Tasks extension or a job ID and status tool) so other clients can launch research.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Hints\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Most quality problems trace back to briefs. Log them and read them first.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Keep page text out of the lead's context: subagents should return quotes and URLs, and the citation pass should re-fetch or read stored page text.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Start the eval with 5 questions while you build, then grow to 20.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>"}]);
